# Positive language example: a biform
# capability cell with independent spec and algorithm evidence.
#
# One cell, two authorities: the `spec:` side carries laws, types, and
# units (what the cell claims); the `algorithm:` side carries reference
# semantics / bytecode (how the claim is computed). Their evidence
# objects are independent — satisfying tests of the algorithm do not
# prove the spec, and a spec proof does not certify a particular
# backend. Softmax here is fixture data: a cell name and evidence
# tokens, never a domain-named Rust branch.
#
# Surface status: admission of the sections below is decided by the
# capability layer's closure planner (crates/emath-ir/src/capability.rs)
# reached from the declaration lane (crates/emath-sema/src/recognition.rs)
# with typed dispositions (provided / refused / not-applicable;
# E-CELL-009 missing side, E-CELL-010 authority escalation, E-CELL-011
# side-evidence collision). The evidence objects are quoted string
# tokens; the class/version/migration rows are the bounded descriptor.
# Exercised by `tests/emath-ir/tests/biform_cells_unit.rs` (as data) and
# `tests/emath-sema/tests/biform_cells.rs` (admission).
package std.math
use std.kinds.capability

emath capability Softmax:
    class: biform
    version: "1.0.0"
    migration: frozen
    inputs:
        x: Vector[Float64]
    outputs:
        probability: Vector[Float64]
    spec:
        # stable-max law: probability = exp(x - max(x)) / sum(exp(x - max(x)))
        evidence: "evidence:std.math.softmax:spec:v1"
    algorithm:
        # reference evaluation under the strict-f64 numeric policy
        evidence: "evidence:std.math.softmax:algorithm:v2"
